[# Section 6 — bindings: C ABI, two-layer model, quickstarts.
   Anchored in spec/bindings.md + spec/abi.md. v0.8.0 ships V/Python/Go/Rust. #]

[section :id bindings :n 6 :title 'Bindings'

  [child :id c-abi :n 6.1 :title 'The C ABI surface'
    [intro
      """libcx is a V library; every other supported language wraps the
         compiled C ABI. The full ABI surface is documented in
         [`spec/abi.md`](../../spec/abi.md); this section is the working
         summary for binding authors."""]

    [body
      """The ABI is intentionally narrow. Every binding sees the same
         function set; capability bits (`cx_features()`) tell a binding
         what optional features the loaded libcx supports."""]

    [list
      [item """**Init.** `cx_init()` is idempotent; every binding calls
              it once at module load."""]
      [item """**Thread registration.** `cx_thread_register()` and
              `cx_thread_unregister()` are required for host-spawned
              threads on bindings whose runtime spawns threads outside
              the V runtime's awareness. Per-binding rules in
              [`spec/abi.md`](../../spec/abi.md) §1.5.5."""]
      [item """**Parse and serialize.** `cx_parse(bytes, len)` and
              `cx_doc_bytes(doc)`."""]
      [item """**Evaluate.** `cx_code_eval(doc, code, target_format)`
              evaluates code against a document. The result is a value
              encoded in the target format (text CX, JSON, etc.)."""]
      [item """**Select.** `cx_select_all(doc, cxpath)` and
              `cx_select(doc, cxpath)` are the CXPath path-value
              entry points (restored in v0.8.0 per ADR
              [0028](../../spec/decisions/0028-cxpath-as-value-kind.md))."""]
      [item """**Modify.** `cx_modify(doc, focus_cxpath, action)`
              returns a new document handle (ADR
              [0030](../../spec/decisions/0030-modify-pure-functional-updates.md))."""]
      [item """**Identity.** `cx_doc_hash(doc)`,
              `cx_doc_equals(a, b)`."""]
      [item """**Capability features.** `cx_features()` returns a
              bitfield. Bindings check before calling new entry
              points."""]]

    [note
      """**Memory model.** libcx uses Boehm GC internally. Binding code
         must not free returned handles directly; it releases them via
         `cx_release()` which decrements the GC root count. Detailed
         lifetime rules are in
         [`spec/abi.md`](../../spec/abi.md) §1.6."""]]

  [child :id layer-1 :n 6.2 :title 'Layer 1 — canonical method set'
    [intro
      """Layer 1 is the **conformance contract**. Every binding implements
         identical method names (case-adjusted to host conventions) with
         identical semantics. Drift between bindings on any Layer-1
         method is a release-blocker (gate 28.6, Layer-1 parity).
         Normative spec: [`spec/bindings.md`](../../spec/bindings.md) §2."""]

    [body
      """**16 canonical methods.** No more, no less, at this layer."""]

    [table :columns (method, returns, description)
      [row :method 'parse(bytes)' :returns 'Doc' :description 'Parse canonical CX bytes']
      [row :method 'Doc.bytes()' :returns 'bytes' :description 'Serialize Doc to canonical CX']
      [row :method 'Doc.hash()' :returns 'string' :description 'SHA-256 of canonical bytes']
      [row :method 'Doc.equals(other)' :returns 'bool' :description 'Canonical-bytes equality']
      [row :method 'Doc.eval(code)' :returns 'Value' :description 'Evaluate CX code against doc']
      [row :method 'Doc.select_all(cxpath)' :returns '[Node]' :description 'CXPath path-value']
      [row :method 'Doc.select(cxpath)' :returns 'Node?' :description 'First match of select_all']
      [row :method 'Doc.modify(focus, action)' :returns 'Doc' :description 'Pure-functional update']
      [row :method 'Doc.find_all(name)' :returns '[Node]' :description 'Name-only convenience']
      [row :method 'Doc.root()' :returns 'Node' :description 'Root element']
      [row :method 'Node.name()' :returns 'string' :description 'Element name']
      [row :method 'Node.attr(name)' :returns 'Value?' :description 'Attribute value']
      [row :method 'Node.attrs()' :returns 'Map' :description 'All attributes']
      [row :method 'Node.children()' :returns '[Node]' :description 'Direct children']
      [row :method 'Node.body()' :returns 'Value' :description 'Element body']
      [row :method 'Node.kind()' :returns 'string' :description 'Item-kind tag']]

    [body
      """CXPath strings are the selector vocabulary across every Layer-1
         selection method. The string is parsed and evaluated by libcx,
         not by the binding — that keeps semantics identical across hosts
         and lets evaluator caching work cross-binding."""]

    [example :lang python
      """# Python — Layer 1
         emails = doc.select_all("//user[@active=true]/@email")
         new_doc = doc.modify("//user[@id=1]/@name", cx.Set("Alice"))"""]

    [example :lang go
      """// Go — Layer 1 (PascalCase per Go style; semantics identical)
         emails, _ := doc.SelectAll("//user[@active=true]/@email")
         newDoc, _ := doc.Modify("//user[@id=1]/@name", cx.Set("Alice"))"""]

    [example :lang rust
      """// Rust — Layer 1
         let emails = doc.select_all("//user[@active=true]/@email")?;
         let new_doc = doc.modify("//user[@id=1]/@name", cx::Set("Alice"))?;"""]

    [example :lang v
      """// V — Layer 1 (native — no wrapper)
         emails := doc.select_all('//user[@active=true]/@email')!
         new_doc := doc.modify('//user[@id=1]/@name', cx.Set('Alice'))!"""]]

  [child :id layer-2 :n 6.3 :title 'Layer 2 — host idiom packs'
    [intro
      """Layer 2 is **opt-in sugar**. Importing it pulls in host-idiomatic
         wrappers that compile to Layer-1 calls at the boundary. The
         compilation is deterministic — every Layer-2 expression has a
         single, documented Layer-1 desugaring. Spec:
         [`spec/bindings.md`](../../spec/bindings.md) §3."""]

    [body
      """Why two layers? Two audiences. **CX-native developers** want CX
         vocabulary in every host language — they get Layer 1.
         **Host-native developers** want list comprehensions, filter
         chains, iterator combinators — they get Layer 2."""]

    [body
      """**Python — `cxlib.idioms`.** List comprehensions, subscript
         assignment, generator over relative paths."""]

    [example :lang python
      """from cxlib import Doc
         from cxlib.idioms import *      # opt-in

         doc = Doc.parse(open("users.cx", "rb").read())

         # List comprehension — desugars to select_all
         active = [u for u in doc if u.tag == "user" and u.attr("active")]
         # ≡ doc.select_all("//user[@active=true]")

         # Subscript with CXPath string
         new_doc = doc.copy()
         new_doc["//user[@id=1]/@name"] = "Alice"
         # ≡ doc.modify("//user[@id=1]/@name", cx.Set("Alice"))

         # Generator over a path
         for email in doc / "//user/@email":
             print(email)
         # ≡ for email in doc.select_all("//user/@email"):"""]

    [body
      """**Go — `cxlib/idioms`.** Builder filter chains, typed
         projections via struct tags."""]

    [example :lang go
      """import "cx/cxlib"
         import . "cx/cxlib/idioms"

         doc, _ := cxlib.Parse(data)

         // Builder chain → CXPath string
         emails := doc.Filter("user").Where("@active=true").Get("/@email")
         // ≡ doc.SelectAll("//user[@active=true]/@email")

         // Typed projection
         type User struct {
             ID    int    \`cx:"@id"\`
             Email string \`cx:"@email"\`
         }
         users := Project[User](doc.Filter("user").Where("@active=true"))"""]

    [body
      """**Rust — `cxlib::idioms`.** Typed iterator combinators,
         `#[derive(CxData)]` for value-typed projections."""]

    [example :lang rust
      """use cxlib::Doc;
         use cxlib::idioms::*;

         let doc = Doc::parse(&data)?;

         let emails: Vec<_> = doc.iter_users()
             .filter(|u| u.active())
             .map(|u| u.email())
             .collect();

         #[derive(CxData)]
         struct User { id: u32, email: String, #[cx(attr)] active: bool }

         let users: Vec<User> = doc.collect::<User>("//user[@active=true]")?;"""]

    [body
      """**V — Layer 1 is already idiomatic.** V is the native reference;
         no separate Layer 2 wrapper. The Layer-1 surface IS the V
         surface."""]

    [note
      """Layer-2 idiom packs are **not** conformance-tested at the API
         level (different bindings have different idioms by design).
         They ARE tested via their documented Layer-1 desugaring —
         `cxlib.idioms.explain(expr)` returns the Layer-1 equivalent
         and is used in fixtures + LSP hovers."""]]

  [child :id schema-codegen :n 6.4 :title 'Schema-driven codegen'
    [intro
      """When a CX schema (`.cxs`, see [[schema]]) is available, every
         binding can generate typed Layer-2 projections from it. This is
         post-v0.8.0 follow-up work; the spec slot is reserved in
         [`spec/bindings.md`](../../spec/bindings.md) §7."""]

    [example :lang cx
      """[# users.cxs #]
         [schema
           [element :name user
             [attribute :name id    :type int    :required true]
             [attribute :name email :type string :required true]
             [attribute :name active :type bool  :default true]]]"""]

    [body
      """`cx schema codegen --target python --in users.cxs --out users.py`
         emits a Python module with a `User` dataclass, type-checked
         Layer-2 accessors, and round-trip serialization. The Rust
         emitter produces `#[derive(CxData)]` structs; the Go emitter
         produces tagged structs."""]]

  [child :id quickstarts :n 6.5 :title 'Per-language quickstarts'
    [intro
      """v0.8.0 ships four bindings: V (native reference), Python, Go,
         and Rust. Others (TypeScript, Java, C#, Ruby, Kotlin, Swift)
         are archived under `lang/_archived/`; see [[migration]]
         for restoration policy."""]

    [child :id v :n 6.5.1 :title 'V (native reference)'
      [body
        """V is the native language of libcx. There is no FFI — V code
           imports the cx module directly. The binding lives in
           `lang/v/native/cxlib/`."""]
      [example :lang v
        """import cx

           fn main() {
             doc := cx.parse(os.read_file('users.cx')!)!
             for user in doc.select_all('//user[@active=true]') {
               println(user.attr('email') or { '<no email>' })
             }
             new_doc := doc.modify('//user[@id=1]/@name', cx.Set('Alice'))!
             println(new_doc.hash())
           }"""]
      [body
        """Errors bubble via V's `!` / `?` propagation. There is no
           Layer 2 — the Layer 1 surface IS V's idiomatic API."""]]

    [child :id python :n 6.5.2 :title 'Python'
      [body
        """Python uses the `cxlib` package; the FFI bridge is in
           `lang/python/cxlib/_native.py`. Layer 2 sugar lives in
           `cxlib.idioms`."""]
      [example :lang python
        """import cxlib as cx

           doc = cx.parse(open('users.cx', 'rb').read())

           for user in doc.select_all('//user[@active=true]'):
               print(user.attr('email'))

           new_doc = doc.modify('//user[@id=1]/@name', cx.Set('Alice'))
           print(new_doc.hash())"""]
      [body
        """Install via `pip install cxlib` (once published). The package
           tracks the parent CX version: v0.8.0 binding requires libcx
           v0.8.0 or compatible (per cap-bit advertisement)."""]]

    [child :id go :n 6.5.3 :title 'Go'
      [body
        """Go uses cgo to call the C ABI. The module lives at
           `cx/cxlib`; Layer 2 sugar at `cx/cxlib/idioms`."""]
      [example :lang go
        """package main

           import (
             "fmt"
             "os"
             "cx/cxlib"
           )

           func main() {
             data, _ := os.ReadFile("users.cx")
             doc, _ := cxlib.Parse(data)
             emails, _ := doc.SelectAll("//user[@active=true]/@email")
             for _, e := range emails { fmt.Println(e) }
             newDoc, _ := doc.Modify("//user[@id=1]/@name", cxlib.Set("Alice"))
             fmt.Println(newDoc.Hash())
           }"""]]

    [child :id rust :n 6.5.4 :title 'Rust'
      [body
        """Rust uses `bindgen` over the C ABI; the crate lives at
           `lang/rust/cxlib/`. Layer 2 in `cxlib::idioms`."""]
      [example :lang rust
        """use cxlib::Doc;
           use std::fs;

           fn main() -> cxlib::Result<()> {
             let data = fs::read("users.cx")?;
             let doc = Doc::parse(&data)?;
             for email in doc.select_all("//user[@active=true]/@email")? {
                 println!("{}", email);
             }
             let new_doc = doc.modify("//user[@id=1]/@name", cxlib::Set("Alice"))?;
             println!("{}", new_doc.hash()?);
             Ok(())
           }"""]
      [body
        """Add to `Cargo.toml`: `cxlib = "0.8.0"`."""]]]]
